<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lhotshampa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lhotshampa are the Nepali-speaking people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>, mostly descended from migrants who settled the southern foothills from the late 19th century. In the late 1980s and early 1990s Bhutan's national identity policies and a citizenship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> led to the departure or expulsion of over 100,000 of them, who lived for years in refugee camps in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> before most were resettled in third countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lhotshampa.jpg" alt="Lhotshampa" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alemaugil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lotshampa_refugees_in_Beldangi_Camp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>People of Nepali origin began to settle in uninhabited areas of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> in the 19th century. Their arrival largely coincided with the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a>, who consolidated power in 1885 after a period of civil unrest and cultivated closer ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Settlers were registered through the agent at Kalimpong, Raja Ugen Dorji, and his son Raja Sonam Togbay Dorji, and immigration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> increased from the 1960s, when Bhutan's first five-year plan brought many as construction workers. The name Lhotshampa, Dzongkha for southern borderlanders, came into official use in the second half of the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The government long tried to limit immigration and to confine Nepalis' homes and work to the south, although measures of the 1970s and 1980s encouraged intermarriage and opened public service. In 1988 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recategorised people of Nepali heritage as illegal immigrants, and local leaders answered with protests demanding citizenship. In 1989 the government made the national dress code, Driglam namzha, compulsory in public during business hours and replaced Nepali with Dzongkha as a language of instruction in schools, which many Lhotshampa did not speak.</p><p>In 1990 unrest and protests in the south pressed for democracy and minority rights, and the Bhutan Peoples' Party, mostly Lhotshampa, began a campaign of violence against the government. Bill Frelick of Human Rights Watch reported that security forces removed Lhotshampa from their homes and coerced them into renouncing citizenship. Many entered seven refugee camps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, which held 85,544 people on 20 January 2010; others worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. From 2007 most of the refugees were resettled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, the United Kingdom and other European countries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Lhotshampa have mostly lived by settled farming, though some cleared forest for slash-and-burn cultivation. They are usually described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, but the Tamang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurung">Gurung</a> are largely Buddhist and the Rai and Limbu largely follow Mundhum. Most abstain from beef, and their main festivals are Dashain and Tihar. Nepali is their first language; since the 1980s it has been taught only at home, so some speakers cannot read or write it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lhotshampa people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The people are mainly Ngalops and Sharchops, with the Nepali-speaking <b>Lhotshampa</b> in the south; two-thirds to three-quarters follow Vajrayana Buddhism and 22.6% are Hindus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
